# Executing Flows

`Jido.Exec` runs canonical Flows through native Runic workflow state. The same
engine supports run-to-completion and step-wise execution.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define Actions And A Flow

```elixir
defmodule FlowExecution.Add do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_execution_add"

  @impl true
  def run(%{left: left, right: right}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{value: left + right}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowExecution.Combine do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_execution_combine"

  @impl true
  def run(%{left: left, right: right}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total: left + right}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowExecution.Diamond do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_execution_diamond"

  flow do
    step "left",
      action: FlowExecution.Add,
      params: %{left: input(:value), right: 1}

    step "right",
      action: FlowExecution.Add,
      params: %{left: input(:value), right: 2}

    step "combine",
      action: FlowExecution.Combine,
      params: %{
        left: result("left", :value),
        right: result("right", :value)
      }

    output(%{result: result("combine")})
  end
end
```

## Run To Completion

```elixir
{:ok, %{result: %{total: 13}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowExecution.Diamond, %{value: 5})
```

Enable concurrent independent work at the call boundary.

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    FlowExecution.Diamond,
    %{value: 5},
    %{},
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

## Start Paused

```elixir
{:ok, execution} =
  Jido.Exec.start(
    FlowExecution.Diamond,
    %{value: 5},
    %{},
    max_concurrency: 2
  )

:running = Jido.Exec.status(execution)
ready = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)
```

`ready` contains native `Runic.Workflow.Runnable` values. It can include
authored components and Runic support work.

## Step One Runnable

```elixir
[runnable | _] = ready

{:ok, %Runic.Workflow.Runnable{} = applied, execution} =
  Jido.Exec.step(execution, runnable.id)

{applied.status, Jido.Exec.status(execution)}
```

`step/1` selects the first ready runnable. `step/2` accepts a ready Runnable or
its integer ID. A work failure can return an applied Runnable with
`status: :failed`.

## Run A Wave

```elixir
{:ok, applied, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)

Enum.map(applied, & &1.status)
```

A wave runs the complete set that is ready when the call begins. Work that
becomes ready waits for the next operation. The wave consumes one execution
revision.

## Continue And Read The Result

```elixir
{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.continue(execution)

:succeeded = Jido.Exec.status(execution)
{:ok, %{result: %{total: 13}}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

Always pass the latest Execution value. Reusing an old revision returns
`Jido.Flow.Error.InvalidExecutionError` before Action work starts.

## Observe Failure

```elixir
defmodule FlowExecution.Fail do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_execution_fail"

  @impl true
  def run(_params, _context), do: {:error, :not_available}
end

defmodule FlowExecution.Failing do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_execution_failing"

  flow do
    step "fail", action: FlowExecution.Fail, params: %{}
    output(%{result: result("fail")})
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:error, error} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowExecution.Failing)
Exception.message(error)
```

A failed runnable stops new dispatch after Jido applies the failure. Work that
already started in the same wave can finish. When multiple wave runnables
fail, the Flow error keeps a canonical failure list.

## Native Work Is Public

Map exposes FanOut, item work, FanIn, and output work. Reduce uses one serial
and resumable authored work unit. Dynamic can add continuation work to the
live graph. Subflow exposes its nested Workflow and InputBinding work. A Join
can enforce explicit or inferred dependencies.

This native surface keeps one scheduler and one graph state. Do not assume
that every ready Runnable is one authored component.

## Execution Lifetime

An Execution is caller-owned in-memory state. It is not a persistent
checkpoint. It cannot resume safely after deployment or process recovery.
Use a higher-level runtime for queues, persistence, retry, cancellation policy,
recovery, and distributed coordination.
